name: finding files
boss: Globber
flavor: Name what you want. I will walk the whole tree.
brief: grep searches inside files. find searches FOR them. It walks a tree and
brief: tests every entry against predicates you chain together.
brief:
brief: Predicates are joined with an implied AND. -o makes it OR, ! negates.
brief: -exec runs a command per result; ending it with + batches many results
brief: into one invocation instead of forking per file.
brief:
brief: Quote your patterns. find . -name *.log lets the SHELL expand the glob
brief: before find ever sees it, which does something else entirely.
---
q: %list% every file and directory under the current directory
a: find .
out: \.
ref: find
teach: find
---
q: %find% every file under here whose name ends in .log
a: find . -name '*.log'
out: \.log
ref: find
teach: find -name
---
q: %find% every .LOG or .log under here, ignoring case in the name
a: find . -iname '*.log'
out: \.log
ref: find
teach: find -iname
---
q: %find% only regular files under here, no directories
a: find . -type f
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -type
hint: f is a regular file, d a directory, l a symlink
---
q: %find% only the directories under here
a: find . -type d
out: \.
ref: find
teach: find -type
---
q: %find% files under here but do not descend more than one level
a: find . -maxdepth 1
out: \.
ref: find
teach: find -maxdepth
---
q: %find% entries under here at least 2 levels deep
a: find . -mindepth 2
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -mindepth
---
q: %find% files under here larger than 1 kilobyte
a: find . -type f -size +1k
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -size
hint: + means "more than", - means "less than", plain means "exactly"
---
q: %find% empty files or directories under here
a: find . -empty
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -empty
---
q: %find% files under here modified in the last day
a: find . -type f -mtime -1
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -mtime
hint: -1 means "less than one day ago"
---
q: %find% files under here modified in the last 30 minutes
a: find . -type f -mmin -30
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -mmin
---
q: %find% files under here that are executable
a: find . -type f -executable
out: \.sh
ref: find
teach: find -executable
---
q: %find% files under here with the exact permission mode 755
a: find . -perm 755
ref: find
teach: find -perm
---
q: %find% files under here owned by user @user@
a: find . -user @user@
ref: find
teach: find -user
---
q: %find% files under here belonging to group @user@
a: find . -group @user@
ref: find
teach: find -group
---
q: %find% either .log or .txt files under here in one command
a: find . -name '*.log' -o -name '*.txt'
out: \.(log|txt)
ref: find
teach: find -o
---
q: %find% files under here that are NOT .log files
a: find . -type f ! -name '*.log'
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -name
---
q: %find% files whose path contains the word src
a: find . -path '*src*'
out: src
ref: find
teach: find -path
---
q: %find% .log files under here but do not descend into the logs directory
a: find . -name logs -prune -o -name '*.log' -print
out: \.log
ref: find
teach: find -prune
hint: -prune means "do not enter this one"; it pairs with -o -print
---
q: stop at the first .log file found rather than walking the whole tree
a: find . -name '*.log' -quit
ref: find
teach: find -quit
---
q: %find% every .log file and run wc -l on each one, one process per file
a: find . -name '*.log' -exec wc -l {} \;
out: [0-9]
ref: find
teach: find -exec
hint: {} is the filename, and the ; ends the command -- escape it from the shell
---
q: %find% every .log file and pass them all to one wc -l instead of one each
a: find . -name '*.log' -exec wc -l {} +
out: [0-9]
ref: find
teach: find -exec
hint: ending with + batches the results; ending with \; forks per file
---
q: %find% files under here without crossing into another filesystem
a: find . -xdev
out: \.
ref: find
teach: find -xdev
---
q: %find% files under here following symlinks rather than listing them
a: find -L . -type f
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -L
---
q: %list% every file under here in depth-first order, contents before the directory
a: find . -depth
out: \.
ref: find
teach: find -depth
---
q: %find% every .log file and print the names separated by NUL rather than newline
a: find . -name '*.log' -print0
out: .
ref: find
teach: find -print0
hint: this is what you pipe into xargs -0 when filenames may contain spaces
